.TH SEQUENCER 2
.SH NAME
sequencer: newsession, init, play \- a CSP-style modular synthesizer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sequencer.m";
sequencer := load Sequencer Sequencer->PATH;
Session: import sequencer;

s := sequencer->newsession();
err := s.cmd("noteon 0 60");
err = s.cmd("play score.ski");
err = s.cmd("stop");
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sequencer
is a CSP-style modular synthesizer: each voice, filter, oscillator,
and envelope is its own process, wired together over typed channels
.RB ( Sample
pulls one block of samples on demand;
.B Control
sends a parameter change) instead of a monolithic audio callback \(em
Limbo's concurrency used as the actual synthesis architecture, not
just plumbing around one.
.PP
Ported from Caerwyn Jones's Inferno lab 62
.RI ( "software synth csp style" ),
.BR https://github.com/caerwynj/inferno-lab/tree/master/62 ;
see SOURCE below for what changed in the port.
.SS "The command language"
A
.B Session
wraps one master
.B Inst
(four independently playable voices, each two-note polyphonic FM
with vibrato, mixed through a shared delay) with a small command
language, the same shape
.IR tk (2)'s
widget
.B cmd
and
.IR krylov (2)'s
own
.B Solver.cmd
already use: one verb-plus-args string per call, string result.
.B Newsession
builds a fresh session (initializing the audio device on first use).
.TP
.BI "noteon " voice " " midinote
Starts note
.I midinote
(standard MIDI note number) on
.I voice
(0\-3).
.TP
.BI "noteoff " voice
Releases the note currently playing on
.IR voice .
.TP
.BI "play " file
Plays a SKINI-format score
.RI ( file ,
"NoteOn dt voice midinote velocity" / "NoteOff dt voice" lines, as in
STK's own score notation) through the session's master
.BR Inst ,
in the background \(em
.B cmd
returns immediately, not when the score finishes. A session only ever
plays one score at a time: calling
.B play
again while one is running is an error, not a silent queue or
interrupt.
.TP
.B stop
Stops a
.B play
in progress. A no-op, not an error, if nothing is playing.
.PP
.B Control's
raw
.RI ( int , real )
pairs and the
.BR CFREQ / CKEYON /...
constants used internally between voices are never exposed through
.BR Session.cmd \(em
the same discipline
.IR pde (2)
uses to keep
.IR krylov (2)'s
own types from crossing its interface.
.SS "Standalone use"
.B Init
is a complete standalone player in its own right (see
.IR playski (1)):
given a single SKINI file as its argument, it builds one session,
plays the file to completion, and exits \(em what a caller wants
.B without
needing the incremental note-by-note or multi-score control
.B Session
gives.
.B Play
is
.BR init 's
own lower-level primitive (drives one score through one already-built
.B Inst
until it ends or "stop" arrives on a caller-supplied channel);
.B Session.cmd's
own
.B play
verb is a thin wrapper over it.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/sequencer.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/sequencer.b
.PP
The DSP graph itself (adsr/mixer/delay/onepole/onezero/twopole/
twozero/lfo/waveloop/fm/master/poly) is unchanged from Caerwyn Jones's
original. Three real adaptations were needed to run in this tree:
.B audioctl()
and the audio device paths, to match this tree's own verified-working
.B /dev/audio
convention (see
.IR tone (2))
rather than the original's
.BR #A/audioctl ;
.B init()
and
.B play()
both called
.B sys->pctl()
through the module-level
.B sys
variable
.B before
.B modinit()
had loaded it \(em a latent bug invisible in whatever environment the
original was written against, but a genuine crash on the very first
line in this build, fixed by loading first; and the
.B Session
command language itself (this file's
.BR cmd / newsession ),
which the original didn't have \(em the original's own GUI
.RI ( gui.b ,
not ported) drove
.B Inst
directly instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
